      "motif": { "channel": "pulse1", "patterns": [1, 2, 10, 11, 13, 16], "variation": "statement over the one drop (p1) - the answer, extended by a beat (p2) - the whole subject re-orchestrated an octave down for A' (p10/p11) - re-harmonised under the chromatic mediant and inverted upward to the global peak in C (p16)" },
      "bank": { "instruments": ["lead-thin", "harm-soft", "echo-thin", "echo-round", "bass", "bass-short", "kick", "snare", "hat-closed", "hat-open", "metal", "steel-comp", "skank", "bubble", "rim"], "rev": 2 },
      "percussionGap": 32,
      "notes": "TROPICAL ANNEX (preset-suite §10.2.2). 8 ROWS PER BEAT, 32 ROWS PER BAR, 2 bars per frame: tempo 150 / speed 6 with rowHighlight 8 gives 24*150/(6*8) = 75 BPM felt, 6 ticks/row, 6.40 s/frame. Even ticks (tempo = 2.5 * engineSpeed), so the groove is dead straight - dub hangs, it does not swing. PERCUSSION GAP 32, justified: frames 10-12 are the dub section and the hats drop out entirely per the brief, leaving kick or rim on row 16/48 plus two ghosts a bar; the longest silence that produces is 17 rows (~1.7 s), which at 8 rows/beat is two beats, not two bars. Every other section keeps hats every 4 rows. DECIMAL PARAMS: 0xy chords are 037 = 55 (minor), 059 = 89 (major 2nd inversion), 038 = 56 (major 1st inversion), 058 = 88 (minor 2nd inversion / minor 1st inversion of the 6th), 007 = 7 (bare fifth - the held tonic dyad under the chromatic bass descent); 442 = 66 (vibrato), 400 = 0 (cancel); A20 = 32 (fade), A00 = 0 (cancel); P7e = 126 (about -10 cents on the echo throw), P80 = 128 (dead centre, restored before the seam); S02 = 2, S03 = 3; D00 = 0; B02 = 2. ECHO DELAY: throws are 6 rows behind the lead, which at 8 rows/beat is the same three-quarters-of-a-beat interval that §10.1's '3 rows' names on green-flash's 4-rows-per-beat grid. ONE DROP ON ONE LANE: the noise channel is monophonic, so the documented 'kick and cross-stick together on beat 3' is realised as kick(36) on row 16 of the first bar and rim(44, noise mode 1, 4-tick envelope) on row 16 of the second - the pair is heard across the two-bar unit rather than stacked, and row 0 stays empty of kick in every bar, which is the whole idea. AXIS 11 EVIDENCE. (a) Structural polyrhythm: a 6-row dotted-8th triangle cell phase-carried across frames 17-19 per §9.1's 64-row table - frame 17 entry row 0 (rows 0,6,12 ... 60, 11 attacks), frame 18 entry row 2 (rows 2,8 ... 62, 11 attacks), frame 19 entry row 4 (rows 4,10 ... 58, 10 attacks) - so the bass argues with the bar for three frames and resolves at frame 20:0. Cite frame 17:0 -> frame 19:58. (b) The bass's dotted-8th + 16th cell (6 rows + 2 rows at this grid) is the piece's rhythmic signature from frame 1:0 on, and is a 3-against-4 unit in its own right. (c) Metric surprise, exactly one: D00 at frame 12:55 drops one beat (8 rows here) at the end of the dub, so A' lands early at frame 13:0. AXIS 7 EVIDENCE. (1) The dub arrangement doctrine: pulse 1 is EMPTY for frames 10, 11 and 12 (pattern 9 has no rows) - melody cut, triangle and noise only, hats out - and the piece is better for it. Three echo throws, each an event at a phrase point, never a running echo: frame 10:6 (echo-thin, e5), frame 11:38 (echo-thin, d5, detuned P7e), frame 12:6 (echo-round, b4, P80 restoring centre). (2) Bass as co-lead: cite frame 10:0 - rows 0, 6, 12, 16, 22, 28 and 32, 38, 44, 48, 54, 60 - the dub bass is the busiest and most interesting line in the piece, which is what makes the room feel bigger rather than emptier. (3) Echo throws inside the song proper, 6 rows behind the lead's phrase ends: frame 2:30 answers the lead at 2:24, frame 2:62 answers 2:56. (4) Comp development: chop on rows 8 and 24 of every bar in A (cite frame 2:8) -> bubble on rows 4,8,12,20,24,28 in B (cite frame 6:4) -> steel-comp offbeats on rows 4,12,20,28 in A' (cite frame 13:4) -> pulse 2 drops the comp entirely and plays a genuine counter-line on harm-soft for the whole of C (cite frame 17:0), which is this piece's §9.2 independence section - 68.1% of pulse 2's attacks fall on rows where pulse 1 has none. WRITTEN DISSONANCES (§9.2). 4-3 suspension: e5 struck at frame 9:24 and held across the chord change into Bm at frame 9:32, where it is the fourth, resolving down by step to d5 - the third - at frame 9:40. Appoggiatura: f#5, the ninth over Em and the phrase's peak, attacked on the strong beat at frame 16:16 and resolving down by step to e5 at frame 16:20; 4 rows is one eighth note here, the same duration §9.2's '1-2 rows' names on a 4-rows-per-beat grid. NON-DIATONIC DEVICES, two, in different sections (§9.3). (1) Chromatic bass descent under a held tonic in B: e2 (frame 6:0) -> d#2 = note 39 (frame 6:32) -> d2 (frame 7:0) -> c#2 (frame 7:32), repeated at frame 8:0 and 8:32, with pulse 2's bubble holding the bare e-b dyad (007 = param 7) and b1 (35) as the stationary common tone in the bass decorations, so the harmony is implied by the collision. (2) Chromatic mediant C major (flat-VI) opening section C at frame 17:0 - triangle c2 (36), pulse 1 g5, pulse 2 g4/c5/e5 - reached from A major at frame 16:32 with e as the common tone. Neither duplicates rust-and-neon's phrygian flat-II. SEQUENCE GOING SOMEWHERE (§9.3): a descending-fifths chain F#m -> Bm -> Em -> A across frames 15-16 (frame 15:0, 15:32, 16:0, 16:32), four links, landing on the chromatic mediant rather than returning home. HARMONIC-RHYTHM CONTRAST (§9.3): A and A' move one chord per bar; section C moves two chords per bar (frame 17:0 C, 17:16 G, 17:32 C, 17:48 Am), and frame 19 prolongs Bm for a whole frame. TWO OR MORE VOICES RESTING (§2.8): frames 10-12 - pulse 1 silent throughout, pulse 2 silent apart from one throw per frame - plus frame 0, where the kit plays alone before the bass enters at frame 1:0."
